            <zone forceUpdate='true' h='47917' id='37' type-v2='text' w='14422' x='85212' y='50391'>
              <formatted-text>
                <run fontsize='8'>Key finding: Contrary to the disparity hypothesis, minority-serving hospitals </run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontsize='8'>(≥50% minority share) receive higher average payments per beneficiary ($24,495) </run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontsize='8'>than low-minority hospitals ($16,707). This is explained by urban concentration — </run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontsize='8'>minority-serving hospitals cluster in high-cost cities where CMS wage index </run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run fontsize='8'>adjustments increase reimbursements. Geography, not race, drives this payment gap.</run>
                <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                <run bold='true' fontsize='8'>Data note: Analysis at hospital level. Patient-level relationships may differ.</run>
              </formatted-text>
